// Practice quiz: Mocking with Mockito
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/testing-and-java-review/mocking-with-mockito

::o1 unstubbed int::A test creates a mock of an interface with int count() and never stubs count. What does count() return? {
~null#Not quite. An int can never be null; null is the default for most object types.
~It throws an exception, because nothing was stubbed#Not quite. A mock accepts every call and returns a default.
=0#Correct. An unstubbed Mockito method returns a default, such as 0 for numbers and false for boolean.
~A random int#Not quite. The default is fixed, so tests stay repeatable.
}

::o1 stub a result::Which line makes prices.price("pen") return 3 in a test, when prices is a mock? {
~verify(prices).price("pen");#Not quite. verify checks that a call happened; it does not set a result.
=when(prices.price("pen")).thenReturn(3);#Correct. when takes the call you want to stub, and thenReturn gives its result.
~prices.price("pen") \= 3;#Not quite. You cannot assign to a method call; Mockito stubs it with when and thenReturn.
~when(prices).thenReturn(3);#Not quite. when needs the method call itself, not just the mock.
}

::o2 default count::What does verify(mailer).send("ada", "Hi") check when it has no second argument? {
~That send was called at least once, with any arguments#Not quite. The arguments must match, and the default count is exactly once.
=That send was called exactly once with those arguments#Correct. verify checks one call by default; times(n) or never() change the count.
~That send was never called#Not quite. That needs never() as the second argument.
~That send returned a value#Not quite. verify checks calls, not results.
}

::o2 a call that must not happen::How do you check that alert() was never called on a mock named notifier? {
=verify(notifier, never()).alert();#Correct. never() as the second argument to verify checks a count of zero.
~verify(notifier).alert();#Not quite. That checks alert was called exactly once.
~when(notifier.alert()).thenReturn(false);#Not quite. That stubs a result; it checks nothing.
~assertFalse(notifier);#Not quite. assertFalse takes a boolean condition, not a mock.
}

::o3 save then count::A LoanService records loans and later counts how many loans a member has. Which double makes its test clearest? {
~A mock with a when line for every count the service will ask for#Not quite. It can work, but the test must script each answer and breaks when the service's calls change.
~A mock checked with verify and times, with no assertion on the count#Not quite. That checks how the code works inside, not whether the count is right.
~The production database#Not quite. That makes it an integration test with setup and shared state.
=A hand-written in-memory fake that stores the loans#Correct. The fake simply remembers each loan, so the test asserts on the final count.
}

::o3 when a mock fits::A test only needs to check that one receipt email was sent. Which double is clearest? {
~A fake mail server that stores every email in a List#Not quite. It works, but it is more code than one verified call needs.
~The real mail service#Not quite. That would send real email and slow the test down.
=A mock of the mailer, checked with verify on the send call#Correct. One outgoing call whose effect has no return value is exactly what verify is for.
~No double; assert on the value that send returns#Not quite. A send method with no return value gives nothing to assert on.
}
